Z||000005||mope||01-30-2001||09:17 PM||||i bought my car and they guy said the clutch was new but still had to put the pedal to the floor...drove it and the clutch went out...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  figured that i should pump it just to get home and i made it.<P>so i bled the clutch and now it works but still reall close to the floor<P>so i checked the pedal for the aluminum part that is supposed to bend....checked with a magnet and everything was steel that i found...<P>have new master, maybe its a bad slave...<P>so im stuck with a sh1tty clutch for now~<P>m0pe||||reg||9
Z||000006||ChadMan||01-31-2001||08:44 AM||||I think $1900 for a low-mileage 85 SE V6 is a good deal, especially if the paint and interior are in good shape.<P>'84 and '85 Fieros had an aluminum clutch pedal that was prone to bending.  '87 and '88 had a steel pedal that fixed this problem (I can't remember what the '86's had - I think aluminum).  This would be your first step to a fix - <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A>  has the replacement pedal and hardware for around $50.<P>Fiero clutches are self-adjusting - there's no manual adjustment.  If the clutch pedal has to be on the floor to shift gears, there's a good chance that either the clutch master cylinder or slave cylinder is bad (probably the slave).  That's a fairly cheap and easy fix, though bleeding the system can be tough.<P>If you end up having to bite the bullet and replace the TO bearing/pressure plate/clutch disc, it'll cost you $700-$800 to have it done by a pro.<P>I don't know how long a Fiero clutch is supposed to last.  Mine had bad hydraulics when I bought it at 66K miles -- replaced the hydraulics myself and had the TO bearing/pp/clutch replaced.  At 118K the clutch feels exactly like it did the day I picked it up from the shop: light pedal effort and smooth engagement near the top of the pedal travel.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ronnie||01-31-2001||04:46 PM||||i just posted a thread last week on my clutch problem.Mine was right down on the floor also.But my clutch pedal was also steel. But I went ahead and replaced the pedal and installed a new bushing in the grommet hole of the master cyl. pushrod.It fixed my clutch problem!!!! Now I have full pedal!!! Fiero Store has the pedal for 32 bucks.<P>------------------<BR>Black '86GT 4spd / Silver '87GT 5spd<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BOTHGTS_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000008||Fformula88||01-31-2001||04:47 PM||||Thanks Chadman!  Thats exactly the info I was looking for  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">  <P>The car physically is in GREAT shape.  It was in a minor front end accident and has a repainted front fascia, hood and left fender.  But it looks to be a very quality job.  I thoroughly looked under in and around and saw no signs of frame bending or other such damage.  The interior is very good except for a hole in the drivers seat side bolster.  I've seen that a million times on Fieros, and a nice seat cover will take care of it.  Its white in color and has some black stripes down the side.  Looks really good.  Plus its a real sleeper with its 84/85 coupe body!  Even more so than my Formula  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>John<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">     <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1987 Fiero Sport Coupe *In rebuilding*<BR>1997 Ford Probe SE<BR>AOL IM: Fformula88||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||01-31-2001||04:50 PM||||Ronnie, would that be considered a rebuild of the master cylinder?  I guess I'll see when we get the car.  Were supposed to pick it up tomorrow as long as the guy who owns it has the title cleared with the bank!  Fiero number 3 in the household  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I cannot WAIT!  hehehe||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ronnie||01-31-2001||05:02 PM||||Nope, not a rebuild.I talking about the eye of the pushrod where it connects to the pedal, under the dash.You don't need any loose play there either.Mine didn't even have any kind of a bushing there, so it had some slop at the pedal.<P>------------------<BR>Black '86GT 4spd / Silver '87GT 5spd<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BOTHGTS_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000011||Neverfinished||01-31-2001||05:19 PM||||I had the same problem in my 85. I overlooked the most simple fix there is...<BR>weld a 1/2 inch piece of steel from your clutch pedal shaft. I don't know the proper lingo for this, sorry. It raises your clutch pedal 1/2 an inch (duh) and creates the perfect clutch. I also read somewhere that the clutch pedal is supposed to be 1/2 an inch higher than the brake pedal, so this should be the way to go. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fformula88||02-02-2001||10:36 AM||||Thanks again everyones imput.  My father picked up the car yesterday, and we will look over the pedal, shaft and stuff this weekend.  ||||reg||1
